This is a big refactor. Let me focus on the actual vulnerability. The issue is a stack-buffer-underflow READ 16 in the `fuzzer_named_arg` target. Let me build the fuzzer and reproduce the crash first, then figure out the input.

Let me look at the `get(name)` function in the vulnerable version again:

```cpp
template <typename Char> format_arg get(basic_string_view<Char> name) const {
    if (!has_named_args()) return {};
    const auto& named_args =
        (is_packed() ? values_[-1] : args_[-1].value_).named_args;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < named_args.size; ++i) {
      if (named_args.data[i].name == name) return get(named_args.data[i].id);
    }
    return {};
  }
```

The stack-buffer-underflow READ 16. Let me think about the `deserialize` function:

```cpp
template <typename Context> basic_format_arg<Context> deserialize() const {
    basic_format_arg<Context> arg;
    std::memcpy(&arg, data, sizeof(basic_format_arg<Context>));
    return arg;
}
```

`data` is `char data[sizeof(basic_format_arg<buffer_context<Char>>)]`. The `deserialize` copies `sizeof(basic_format_arg<Context>)` bytes from `data`. If `Context` is `format_context` and `buffer_context<Char>` is also `format_context`, then sizes match. But if `Context` is a different context (e.g., `wformat_context`), sizes could differ.

Actually, let me reconsider. The stack-buffer-underflow READ 16. Let me look at the `named_arg` structure:

```cpp
template <typename T, typename Char>
struct named_arg : view, named_arg_base<Char> {
  const T& value;
  named_arg(basic_string_view<Char> name, const T& val)
      : named_arg_base<Char>(name), value(val) {}
};
```

And `named_arg_base`:
```cpp
template <typename Char> struct named_arg_base {
  basic_string_view<Char> name;
  mutable char data[sizeof(basic_format_arg<buffer_context<Char>>)];
  named_arg_base(basic_string_view<Char> nm) : name(nm) {}
  template <typename Context> basic_format_arg<Context> deserialize() const {
    basic_format_arg<Context> arg;
    std::memcpy(&arg, data, sizeof(basic_format_arg<Context>));
    return arg;
  }
};
```

Now, the `deserialize` is called on `named_arg_base`. But `named_arg` inherits from `named_arg_base`. The `data` array is at the start of `named_arg_base`, followed by `value` in `named_arg`. 

Wait, actually the issue might be in how `deserialize` is called. Let me look at where `deserialize` is called in the vulnerable version. In `get(int id)`:

```cpp
format_arg get(int id) const {
    format_arg arg = do_get(id);
    if (arg.type_ == internal::type::named_arg_type)
      arg = arg.value_.named_arg->template deserialize<Context>();
    return arg;
}
```

And in `arg_map::push_back`:
```cpp
void push_back(value<Context> val) {
    const auto& named = *val.named_arg;
    map_[size_] = {named.name, named.template deserialize<Context>()};
    ++size_;
}
```

Hmm. Let me focus on the actual crash. Let me build the fuzzer and reproduce. Let me check the build setup.

